For this lab, I took the machine learning methods to predict which couples violated the 'half your age plus seven' rule.
I used household income, geographic region, employment status, and hours worked as my explanatory variables.
I evaluated the models by comparing their predicted classifications with the actual outcomes in my test dataset. I calculated accuracy by dividing the number of correctly classified observations by the total number of observations in the dataset.
I found that OLS, logistic regression, and LASSO had the highest predictive accuracy at 98.7%. 
Random forest and SVM performed almost identically, also with 98.7% accuracy. Ridge had the lowest accuracy at 98.6%.
I also compared the false positives and false negatives across the models to better understand their predictive performance.
I found that all the models had zero false positives, meaning that every couple predicted to violate the guideline actually did. However, the models had a large number of false negatives.
Looking at the F1 scores- OLS, logistic regression, and LASSO had the highest F1 score of 0.163, followed by random forest at 0.160 and SVM at 0.159. Ridge had an F1 score of 0.

I wanted to see whether the more advanced methods provided any additional predictive benefit over the OLS and logistic regressions.
LASSO did not improve predictive accuracy compared with OLS or logistic regression. All three models achieved the same accuracy of 98.7% and produced identical numbers of false positives and false negatives.
Ridge showed the largest decline in accuracy, predicting 0.13% worse than OLS and logistic regression. Ridge also failed to correctly identify any violations.

Using random forest variable- I found that household income was the most important predictor, with an importance score of 30.81. Hours worked was the second most important variable, with a score of 19.40.
Overall, I found that OLS, logistic regression, and LASSO all performed the best. The more complicated machine learning methods do not automatically produce better predictions.
